I am thinking...: Looking at `xmlUTF8Strlen`:

```c
while (*utf != 0) {
    if (utf[0] & 0x80) {
        if ((utf[1] & 0xc0) != 0x80)
            return(-1);
        if ((utf[0] & 0xe0) == 0xe0) {
            if ((utf[2] & 0xc0) != 0x80)
                return(-1);
            if ((utf[0] & 0xf0) == 0xf0) {
                if ((utf[0] & 0xf8) != 0xf0 || (utf[3] & 0xc0) != 0x80)
                    return(-1);
                utf += 4;
            } else {
                utf += 3;
            }
        } else {
            utf += 2;
        }
    } else {
        utf++;
    }
    ret++;
}
```

If the last byte is a 2-byte lead (0xC0-0xDF), it checks `utf[1] & 0xc0 != 0x80`. Since utf[1] is the null terminator (0x00), `0x00 & 0xc0 = 0x00 != 0x80`, so it returns -1. This is still within bounds (the null terminator byte is allocated).

If the last byte is a 3-byte lead (0xE0-0xEF), it first checks `utf[1] & 0xc0 != 0x80`. utf[1] is 0x00, which fails the check, so it returns -1. Again, only reading one byte past the data (the null terminator).

Actually wait - the buffer is `ret_len + 1` bytes. If the lead byte is at index `ret_len - 1`:
- utf[0] = byte at index ret_len-1 (the lead byte)
- utf[1] = byte at index ret_len (the null terminator, within bounds)
- utf[2] = byte at index ret_len+1 (OUT OF BOUNDS!)

But the check on utf[1] happens first: `(utf[1] & 0xc0) != 0x80`. Since utf[1] is 0x00, this evaluates to true, so it returns -1 before ever reading utf[2]. So xmlUTF8Strlen won't trigger the OOB read.

